description:
Write an in-memory model of the registry API from lesson 5.02, with its real error codes:

```cpp
constexpr long ERROR_SUCCESS = 0, ERROR_FILE_NOT_FOUND = 2, ERROR_UNSUPPORTED_TYPE = 1630;
enum class Type { String, Dword, MultiString };

class Registry
{
public:
    long setString(const std::string& key, const std::string& name, const std::string& value);
    long setDword(const std::string& key, const std::string& name, std::uint32_t value);
    long setMulti(const std::string& key, const std::string& name, const std::vector<std::string>& values);
    long getString(const std::string& key, const std::string& name, std::string& out) const;
    long getDword(const std::string& key, const std::string& name, std::uint32_t& out) const;
    long getMulti(const std::string& key, const std::string& name, std::vector<std::string>& out) const;
    long deleteValue(const std::string& key, const std::string& name);
    long deleteTree(const std::string& key);
    std::vector<std::string> subkeys(const std::string& key) const;
};

std::string encodeMulti(const std::vector<std::string>& values);   // REG_MULTI_SZ bytes (one byte per character)
std::vector<std::string> decodeMulti(const std::string& data);
```

- Keys are paths like `Software\Xenon\Notes`; **key and value names are case-insensitive** but keep the case they were first created with. Setting a value creates the key and all its parents.
- Reading a value that is missing, or from a key that does not exist, is `ERROR_FILE_NOT_FOUND`; reading with the wrong type is `ERROR_UNSUPPORTED_TYPE` (and `out` is left unchanged in both cases).
- Setting a value of another type replaces it.
- `deleteTree` removes a key with all its subkeys (`ERROR_FILE_NOT_FOUND` if it does not exist). `subkeys` lists the direct subkeys of a key in their stored case, sorted ignoring case (an unknown key has none).
- `encodeMulti`: each string followed by a null, then one more null; an empty list is two nulls. Empty strings cannot be stored in a multi-string (they would end it early), so they are skipped. `decodeMulti` reads up to the first empty string.

